#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""bench: build an engine's harness, run it the way antirez's ds4 rules ask, read the result.

  bench build <commit|dir> <out> [--cuda] [--engine qwen36] [--repo <git dir>] [--make VAR=VAL]
  bench run <out> <version> [<version>] [options]          (bench run -h lists them)
  bench report <out>
  bench manifest <out> --metric prompt|decode --commit <sha> --hypothesis "..." --evidence <file> [--quality "..."]

A version is [label=]<harness binary|commit|source dir>[,VAR=VAL...]: two binaries, or one binary with and
without a variable, or two commits that `bench run` builds first. The defaults are the measuring rules of
docs/guida-benchmark.md: the first 64 and 512 tokens of I Promessi Sposi, 32 teacher-forced tokens after
each, a warm-up run that does not count, then 3 runs (4 per version with two, in ABBA order: a machine that
slows as it heats weighs the same on both), each a new process after the machine is cool and a 60 s rest.

The driver knows nothing of a model's insides: it runs a harness that prints the CSV contract of README.md,
on macOS (Metal) or Linux (CUDA). What depends on the system is in host_state(), cool() and time_cmd(); what
depends on the engine is a line of ENGINES. Python 3 standard library only."""
